-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Chunk coordinates are parsed from strings. Ensure consistent formatting (e.g., no spaces) to avoid parsing errors [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundaries:** The `removeDistance` parameter is critical. If set too low, chunks may flicker in and out [G]. If set too high, memory usage increases [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Degenerate Input:** If `pending_chunks` is empty, `count` becomes 0, and the loop does not execute [S1, unofficial]. This is handled correctly by the `max(1, ...)` logic only if `asyncChunkGeneration` is true and `count` is not pre-set [G]. If `asyncChunkGeneration` is false, `count` is 0, and the loop is skipped, which is correct [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Chunk Lifecycle Management
-Handles the full lifecycle of a chunk from creation to disposal, including caching and reference counting. Choose this when you need fine-grained control over chunk persistence beyond simple distance-based removal. [Chunk Lifecycle Management](3987-chunk-lifecycle-management.md)
+### Chunked Async Reading
+Reads data in chunks to fill a buffer asynchronously. Reserves 8 MB in buffer, loops reading into buffer until capacity is reached or EOF, yielding after each read [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Loading is $O(K)$ per frame, where $K$ is the number of chunks processed (typically 10% of pending) [S1, unofficial]. Removal is $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of loaded chunks, as it iterates through all chunks to check distance [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$, where $M$ is the number of chunks within `removeDistance` of the player [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources. [G]
-*   **Scaling:** Scales well with large worlds as only nearby chunks are loaded. Performance depends on the cost of `generateChunk` and `voxelMesh` [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Prevents frame drops by spreading generation load over multiple frames. Reduces memory footprint by unloading distant chunks [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Not given in the sources. [G]
-*   **When to use:** When rendering large voxel worlds in real-time applications (e.g., games) where memory and frame rate are critical [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** For small, static worlds where all data can be loaded at once [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Loading is throttled to $O(1)$ or $O(k)$ per frame where $k$ is small (e.g., 1-16 chunks), preventing frame spikes [S1, S3, S4, unofficial]. Unloading is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of loaded chunks, but typically $N$ is small relative to the total world.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is proportional to the render volume ($\text{RenderRadius}^2 \times \text{ChunkSize}^3$) [S2, S4, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Prevents stuttering during camera movement or large jumps by spreading generation cost over multiple frames [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of `removeDistance` and `renderRadius` to balance memory and latency. Spherical worlds add complexity with distance checks against planet radius [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Large open worlds where the player moves continuously. Not suitable for small, static scenes where synchronous loading is simpler.
 
 ## Background
-This method originates from the `voxel-engine` library by max-mapper, an unofficial implementation of a voxel world engine for JavaScript [S1, unofficial]. It is designed to work with Three.js for rendering and gl-matrix for vector operations [S1, unofficial]. The approach is common in voxel-based games like Minecraft to handle vast, procedurally generated worlds [G].
+Originating from voxel game engines like `voxel-engine` [S1, S2, unofficial] and extended to spherical planet rendering in projects like `AzureVoxel` [S3, unofficial] and `VoxelEngine` [S4, unofficial], this method addresses the scalability limits of in-memory voxel storage. It evolved from simple distance-based culling to include asynchronous generation and meshing to maintain frame rates [S1, S3, unofficial].
 
